From 802b38d3a56d11d7cac2169d6b59ab763b99259b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: duncan0k <dev@ark0.si>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 09:13:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 108/112] build: add signet-slh-sign, the SLH-DSA block signer
 of a signet block producer

A small C program, src/signet-slh-sign.c, over the vendored slhdsa-c the node
verifies with: it links the objects of bitcoin_slhdsa128s, compiled with that
library's options, and nothing else of Core; no node binary links it. Built
with BUILD_SIGNET_SLH_SIGNER (the default when tests are built), on Linux only:
it relies on prctl(PR_SET_DUMPABLE) and getrandom. It is linked fully static
and not position-independent (-static -no-pie; libc.a from libc6-dev): it loads
no loader and no library, so a release check has only its one file to read.

  signet-slh-sign <keyfile>           the 32-byte message as 64 hexadecimal
                                      characters on standard input (an optional
                                      final newline); the signature in hex
  signet-slh-sign -pubkey <keyfile>   the public key in hex

<keyfile> holds exactly the 48 bytes SK.seed || SK.prf || PK.seed. The
program reads and checks the message before it opens the key file, signs with
FIPS 205 slh_sign, context SIGNET/SLHBLOCK/EXP0 and a fresh 16-byte opt_rand
from getrandom, verifies the signature with the public key, and prints it
only then. On a non-zero exit standard output must be ignored: it is empty
unless writing the result itself failed part way.

The secret: before the key file is opened the process makes itself
non-dumpable (PR_SET_DUMPABLE 0, checked with PR_GET_DUMPABLE; exit 6
otherwise), so no core dump is written (do_coredump in fs/coredump.c stops
first for such a process, whatever core_pattern says). The key is read with
open and read into the program's own buffer, no stdio. Every buffer of its own
that held the key or a derived secret is overwritten on every return path,
and after key generation, signing and verification 64 KiB of stack below the
caller is overwritten through volatile writes (the library uses under 9 KiB;
it is not changed, as the node compiles it too). Registers and the pages the
kernel reclaims at exit remain, which a C program cannot clear.

signet-slh-sign-fault-test, built only with the tests and never installed, is
the same program with the faults SIGNET_SLH_SIGN_TEST_FAULT selects: a bit of
every signature flipped between signing and verifying, or a failing random
source.

The stack scrub holds for an optimised build without sanitizers or split
stacks, the only kind that ships: AddressSanitizer's fake stack, SafeStack's
unsafe stack and GCC's split stacks move frames where it cannot reach. So
CMake builds no signet-slh-sign when SANITIZERS is set, or when -fsanitize or
-fsplit-stack appears in the compiler arguments (CMAKE_<LANG>_COMPILER_ARG1),
the final C, C++ and executable linker flags (per configuration too), Core's
APPEND_* flags or the options of the SLH library and sanitize_interface. The
decision is taken in one place, where the targets are made, after every
language is enabled (so CFLAGS and CXXFLAGS count), and test/config.ini is
written after src/ from it; the configure output says why the signer was left
out. Only the test option SIGNET_SLH_SIGN_ALLOW_SANITIZERS builds a separate
target with SANITIZERS, signet-slh-sign-sanitizer-test, with no install rule
and marked with the string SIGNET_SLH_SIGN_SANITIZER_BUILD for release staging
to refuse; the source does not compile under the sanitizers a compiler
announces (address, hwaddress, memory, thread, safe-stack) except as that
target. No compiler announces -fsplit-stack.
config.ini says which of the three programs exist.
